  <h2>What a concept actually is</h2>
  <p>Ask ten students for their concept and you'll get ten nouns: &ldquo;connection&rdquo;, &ldquo;flow&rdquo;, &ldquo;nature&rdquo;, &ldquo;community&rdquo;. None of those is a concept &mdash; they're themes, and a theme can't tell you where to put a wall. A real architectural concept is an <strong>organising idea with consequences</strong>. It answers &ldquo;what is this building fundamentally doing?&rdquo; in a way that makes design decisions for you.</p>
  <p>Test it like this: if your concept is true, some things become obvious and others become forbidden. &ldquo;The building is a series of thresholds that slow you down as you move inward&rdquo; is a concept &mdash; it dictates circulation, compression and release, where light is withheld and given. &ldquo;Sustainability&rdquo; is not; it's a value every building should hold, but it doesn't shape the plan. The difference is <em>generative power</em>. A good concept is a decision-making machine.</p>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Studio tip.</strong> Finish this sentence: &ldquo;This building is really about ______, so it must ______ and must never ______.&rdquo; If you can fill all three blanks, you have a concept. If only the first, you have a theme.</div>

  <h2>Where concepts come from</h2>
  <p>Concepts don't arrive by staring at a blank page. They emerge from tension &mdash; the friction between the brief, the site and your own interests. Mine those three sources deliberately.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>From the site.</strong> Your <a href="/process/site-analysis/">design driver</a> often <em>is</em> the seed of a concept &mdash; a slope, a view, a boundary that wants a particular response.</li>
    <li><strong>From the brief.</strong> The intentions and adjectives you pulled out when <a href="/process/how-to-read-a-brief/">reading the brief</a> point to what the building should <em>feel</em> like.</li>
    <li><strong>From a question.</strong> The strongest concepts often start as &ldquo;what if?&rdquo; &mdash; what if the roof were the ground, what if the corridor were the main event, what if the building had no front?</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Generate widely, then commit</h2>
  <p>The mistake is falling in love with your first idea. Force quantity before quality: sketch ten concept diagrams in an hour, each a different bet, none precious. Bad ideas are useful &mdash; they map the edges of the good ones. Work small and fast, in thumbnails and single sections, so you can throw them away without pain. Only once you have breadth should you narrow. Divergence first, then convergence &mdash; hold that discipline and your final concept will be a choice, not an accident.</p>

  <h2>Test every idea against something</h2>
  <p>A concept you haven't tested is just a hope. Put each candidate under pressure before you invest weeks in it.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Against the brief.</strong> Does it deliver the requirements and honour the intentions, or does it fight them?</li>
    <li><strong>Against the site.</strong> Does the site make it stronger, or would it work anywhere (a bad sign)?</li>
    <li><strong>Against experience.</strong> Walk through it in your head. What do you see, feel, and do at the door, in the middle, at the end?</li>
    <li><strong>Against a section.</strong> Concepts that only work in plan are usually weak. Draw the section &mdash; if the idea survives there too, it's real.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>From concept to parti</h2>
  <p>The <strong>parti</strong> is your concept boiled down to its irreducible diagram &mdash; the single organising move drawn so simply a stranger could grasp it in seconds. If the concept is &ldquo;thresholds that slow you down&rdquo;, the parti might be a row of parallel walls with a narrowing path threading through them. Getting to a parti is an act of ruthless subtraction: keep removing until only the idea remains. When you can draw your whole scheme as one clean diagram and still explain the project from it, you have a parti &mdash; and everything you design afterward should stay loyal to it. If a later decision contradicts the parti, either the decision is wrong or your concept has evolved and the parti needs redrawing. Both are fine; drift without noticing is not.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The label concept.</strong> A word stuck on a finished building to justify it after the fact. Tutors can smell this instantly.</li>
    <li><strong>The literal concept.</strong> A library shaped like a book, a museum of the sea shaped like a wave. Metaphor is not organisation.</li>
    <li><strong>The abandoned concept.</strong> A strong idea in week two that has vanished by the final crit. Consistency is more convincing than cleverness.</li>
    <li><strong>The un-testable concept.</strong> So vague it can never be wrong, and therefore never useful.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout warn"><strong>Common trap.</strong> Designing the building first and inventing the concept afterward to explain it. It always reads as a costume. Let the concept lead &mdash; even a modest idea, followed honestly through plan, section and material, produces a more coherent project than a brilliant idea bolted on at the end.</div>

